%S A400825 2,6,10,12,16,30,60,64,94,118,120,138,178,200,226,738,798,808,1010,
%T A400825 1140,1384,1494,1696,4620,5672,54890,75480
%N A400825 Numbers k such that (2^k-1)*2^(k+4)+1 is prime.
%C A400825 Largest primes have been shown prime using the Lucas-Lehmer-Riesel test (see Wright link).
%C A400825 Let N = N(k) = (2^k-1)*2^(k+4)+1. Conjecture: If 2^((N-1)/4) == -1 (mod N), then N is prime.
%H A400825 Gary Wright, <a href="https://mathrelated.co.uk/index.php/2026/10/05/type-1-plus-4-proving-prime/">Type 1 plus 4 - proving prime</a>, Oct 05 2026.
%H A400825 Gary Wright, <a href="https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/5151272/prime-test-for-proth-numbers-generalised-is-it-valid">Prime test for Proth Numbers - generalised</a>, Oct 07 2026.
%F A400825 a(n) = A316360(n) - 2. - _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 12 2026
%t A400825 q[k_] := PrimeQ[(2^k-1)*2^(k+4)+1]; Select[Range[2, 2000, 2], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 12 2026 *)
%Y A400825 Cf. A000225, A316360, A400454.
%K A400825 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A400825 1,1
